<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S5">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experimental Setup</h2><b=
utton class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for pr=
eceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">We start from two code generation benchmarks and apply o=
ur dataset construction method (Section <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https:=
//arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#S4" title=3D"4. Dataset Construction =E2=80=
=A3 When Prompts Go Wrong: Evaluating Code Model Robustness to Ambiguous, C=
ontradictory, and Incomplete Task Descriptions"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx=
_ref_tag">4</span></a>) to produce unclear task descriptions. To see how th=
ese changes affect model performance, we fed the altered task descriptions =
to multiple code generation models and evaluate the correctness of the code=
 outputs using the test cases available in the original benchmark.</p><butt=
on class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for prece=
ding element</button>
</div>
<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S5.SS1">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Original Benchmarks<=
/h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue =
for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">We selected two widely recognized code generation benchm=
arks <cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Dakhel et&nbsp;al., <a c=
lass=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#bib.bib11" tit=
le=3D"">2023</a>)</cite>, each offering distinct characteristics and challe=
nges, as shown in Table&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org=
/html/2507.20439v1#S4.T1" title=3D"Table 1 =E2=80=A3 4.2. Dataset Validatio=
n =E2=80=A3 4. Dataset Construction =E2=80=A3 When Prompts Go Wrong: Evalua=
ting Code Model Robustness to Ambiguous, Contradictory, and Incomplete Task=
 Descriptions"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Both benc=
hmarks contain task descriptions, a reference (and correct) code solution, =
and test cases to evaluate that the generated code corresponds to the task =
description.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">R=
eport issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS1.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MBPP</span> <ci=
te class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Austin et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"=
ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#bib.bib6" title=3D"">2=
021a</a>)</cite> comprises 974 crowd-sourced Python programming problems, d=
esigned to be approachable for entry-level programmers.</p><button class=3D=
"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding elemen=
t</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS1.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HumanEval</span=
> <cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Chen et&nbsp;al., <a class=
=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#bib.bib10" title=
=3D"">2021b</a>)</cite> consists of 164 hand-crafted Python programming pro=
blems. The task descriptions systematically include a function signature, a=
 functional description, and several illustrative example outputs.</p><butt=
on class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for prece=
ding element</button>
</div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S5.SS2">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Evaluation Metrics <=
/h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue =
for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">To answer RQ1, we report the Matthews Correlation Coeffi=
cient (MCC) for each model to classify task descriptions into clear (origin=
al) or unclear (mutated), and to classify each unclear task description int=
o the three defined categories.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=
=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS2.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">For code generation, we use the following metrics <cite =
class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Chai and Draxler, <a class=3D"ltx_r=
ef" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#bib.bib7" title=3D"">2014</=
a>)</cite>:</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Re=
port issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<section class=3D"ltx_paragraph" id=3D"S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1">
<h4 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pass@1</h4><button class=3D"sr-=
only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</b=
utton>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">The percentage of tasks for which the model=E2=80=99s to=
p-1 solution program passes all test cases <cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_cite=
macro_citep">(Yetistiren et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://=
arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#bib.bib42" title=3D"">2022</a>)</cite>.</p><but=
ton class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for prec=
eding element</button>
</div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_paragraph" id=3D"S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2">
<h4 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Successful Execution Rate (SER)=
</h4><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue=
 for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">The percentage of programs generated that run without er=
rors, regardless of correctness.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=
=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_paragraph" id=3D"S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3">
<h4 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"> Runnable but Incorrect Rate (R=
IR) </h4><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">Among those that execute successfully, the share that fa=
ils at least one test case.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"di=
splay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S5.SS3">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Models, Parameters, =
and Infrastructure</h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: n=
one;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS3.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">We consider four state=E2=80=91of=E2=80=91the=E2=80=91ar=
t code generation LLMs <cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Uc-Cet=
ina, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#bib.b=
ib36" title=3D"">2023</a>)</cite>: DeepSeek <cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_cit=
emacro_citep">(AI, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507=
.20439v1#bib.bib3" title=3D"">2024</a>)</cite>, CodeLlama <cite class=3D"lt=
x_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Roziere et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=
=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#bib.bib32" title=3D"">2023</a>)</ci=
te>, GPT=E2=80=914 <cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(OpenAI, <a=
 class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#bib.bib25" t=
itle=3D"">2023</a>)</cite>, and Qwen=E2=80=912.5 <cite class=3D"ltx_cite lt=
x_citemacro_citep">(Qin et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://a=
rxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#bib.bib27" title=3D"">2024</a>)</cite>. For open=
 source families, we evaluated both a smaller variant <span class=3D"ltx_te=
xt ltx_font_italic">(deepseek-coder-6.7B-instruct, CodeLlama-7B-instruct, a=
nd Qwen2.5-7B-instruct)</span> and a larger variant <span class=3D"ltx_text=
 ltx_font_italic">(DeepSeekCode-33B-Instruct, CodeLlama-34B-Instruct, and Q=
wen2.5-32B-Instruct)</span>. GPT=E2=80=914 is accessed via the OpenAI API i=
n its default configuration. All open source checkpoints were obtained from=
 Hugging Face.<span class=3D"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote" id=3D"footnote2"><=
sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class=3D"ltx_note_outer"><span cla=
ss=3D"ltx_note_content"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class=3D"=
ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span><a class=3D"ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewrit=
er" href=3D"https://huggingface.co/" title=3D"">https://huggingface.co/</a>=
</span></span></span></p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display:=
 none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS3.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">For code generation, we set the maximum sequence length =
to 512 tokens and evaluate each model=E2=80=99s outputs using the test suit=
e provided by the original benchmark.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" s=
tyle=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.SS3.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">All experiments were run on a Linux server equipped with=
 four Intel Xeon Silver 4416+ CPUs and four NVIDIA L40S GPUs (46 GB memory =
each).
Our code and data are publicly available <span class=3D"ltx_note ltx_role_f=
ootnote" id=3D"footnote3"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class=
=3D"ltx_note_outer"><span class=3D"ltx_note_content"><sup class=3D"ltx_note=
_mark">3</sup><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>https://github.c=
om/serval-uni-lu/Robustness-of-LLMs-to-prompt-imperfections</span></span></=
span>.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report =
issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S6">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Results</h2><button class=
=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding ele=
ment</button>
<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S6.SS1">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>RQ1 =E2=80=93 Task d=
escription classification</h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"dis=
play: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
<figure class=3D"ltx_figure" id=3D"S6.F4"><img alt=3D"Refer to caption" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height=3D"297" id=3D"S6=
.F4.g1" src=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1/images/mcc.png" width=3D=
"479">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Classification accuracy (%) of LLMs on identif=
ying task description issues in two benchmarks. The top row shows results o=
n MBPP, the bottom on HumanEval. From left to right: (1) binary accuracy fo=
r distinguishing clear versus unclear task descriptions; (2) multiclass acc=
uracy for identifying task descriptions as incomplete, ambiguous, or contra=
dictory; and (3) per=E2=80=90category accuracy broken down by each type of =
task description imperfection.</figcaption>
</figure><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">To evaluate the ability of LLMs to distinguish clear tas=
k descriptions from unclear ones, we randomly selected 300 tasks from the o=
riginal benchmarks and 300 mutated task descriptions (100 from each of the =
ambiguous, contradictory, and incomplete categories), thus ensuring balance=
d sample sizes. Subsequently, each evaluated model is prompted to classify =
every task description as clear or unclear. For the mutated task descriptio=
ns, we further ask the model to classify them into one of the three issue c=
ategories.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Rep=
ort issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS1.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">Figure <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/ht=
ml/2507.20439v1#S6.F4" title=3D"Figure 4 =E2=80=A3 6.1. RQ1 =E2=80=93 Task =
description classification =E2=80=A3 6. Results =E2=80=A3 When Prompts Go W=
rong: Evaluating Code Model Robustness to Ambiguous, Contradictory, and Inc=
omplete Task Descriptions"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a=
> shows the classification results for seven LLMs, including GPT-4, Qwen, D=
eepSeek Coder, and CodeLlama, in both their small (6=E2=80=937B) and large =
(32=E2=80=9334B) variants.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"dis=
play: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS1.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">The MCC score of the evaluated LLMs in classifying task =
descriptions as =E2=80=98clear=E2=80=99 or =E2=80=98unclear=E2=80=99 ranges=
 from -0.06 to 0.47 on HumanEval and from -0.1 to 0.45 on MBPP, with GPT=E2=
=80=914 and both Qwen variants (7B and 32B) achieving the highest values in=
 each dataset. When we ask LLMs to assign each unclear task description to =
one of the three defined categories (ambiguous, incomplete, and contradicto=
ry), GPT4 again leads, recording an MCC of 0.55 on HumanEval and 0.45 on MB=
PP, followed by Qwen32B at 0.50 and 0.42, respectively; all other models fa=
ll between =E2=80=930.10 and 0.30.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" styl=
e=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS1.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">All the LLMs, except Deepseek variants, classify =E2=80=
=98Contradictory=E2=80=99 descriptions best, then =E2=80=98Incomplete=E2=80=
=99, while =E2=80=98Ambiguous=E2=80=99 descriptions are hardest. Deepseek m=
odels do not follow a clear order. Overall, =E2=80=98Ambiguous=E2=80=99 des=
criptions remain challenging for almost every model.</p><button class=3D"sr=
-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</=
button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS1.p5">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">Overall, our evaluation reveals that language models lik=
e GPT-4 demonstrate only modest capability in identifying unclear task desc=
riptions. Even the best-performing LLMs attain an MCC of approximately 0.50=
. These models also often fail to pinpoint the issues in a task description=
; deeply ambiguous cases, where multiple interpretations are equally valid,=
 are the hardest for all architectures tested. Additionally, when prompting=
 the LLMs to explain the issues, they failed badly as in almost all cases w=
hat they pointed out was unclear or irrelevant.=20
<br class=3D"ltx_break"></p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"displ=
ay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S6.SS1.p6">
<p class=3D"ltx_p ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style=3D"width:433.6pt;"><=
span class=3D"ltx_text" style=3D"background-color:#ECECEC;">
<span class=3D"ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style=3D"width=
:420.8pt;">
<span class=3D"ltx_p"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1 Results.</=
span>
Code generation models cannot reliably detect problems in task descriptions=
. This shows that, unlike humans, they cannot natively react to low-quality=
 requirements and would nevertheless attempt to produce a solution.</span>
</span></span></p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;"=
>Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S6.SS2">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>RQ2 =E2=80=93 Model =
Robustness</h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Re=
port issue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">The fact that code generation models cannot detect uncle=
ar task descriptions motivate us to study their ability to nevertheless pro=
duce the expected code. To assess this robustness, we compare their perform=
ance on both HumanEval and MBPP when they are confronted to original, clear=
 task descriptions versus the unclear task descriptions that we generate.</=
p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue fo=
r preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS2.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">Table&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.or=
g/html/2507.20439v1#S6.T2" title=3D"Table 2 =E2=80=A3 6.2. RQ2 =E2=80=93 Mo=
del Robustness =E2=80=A3 6. Results =E2=80=A3 When Prompts Go Wrong: Evalua=
ting Code Model Robustness to Ambiguous, Contradictory, and Incomplete Task=
 Descriptions"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Table&=
nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#S6.T3=
" title=3D"Table 3 =E2=80=A3 6.2. RQ2 =E2=80=93 Model Robustness =E2=80=A3 =
6. Results =E2=80=A3 When Prompts Go Wrong: Evaluating Code Model Robustnes=
s to Ambiguous, Contradictory, and Incomplete Task Descriptions"><span clas=
s=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show the results on HumanEval and M=
BPP, respectively. Across both data sets and all models, Pass@1 performance=
 decreases significantly when faced with unclear task descriptions. On aver=
age, ambiguous descriptions lead to a 25=E2=80=9330% reduction in Pass@1 ac=
curacy, while incomplete descriptions cause drops of 20 to 25%. Contradicto=
ry descriptions have the most severe impact, reducing accuracy by up to 40%=
. For example, GPT-4 achieves 73.8% Pass@1 on original HumanEval descriptio=
ns, but only 6.7% on contradictory ones. Similar trends are observed with Q=
wen-32B and DeepSeek-33B, indicating that even the most capable models are =
sensitive to quality issues in task descriptions.</p><button class=3D"sr-on=
ly button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</but=
ton>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS2.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">A similar trend appears when considering the semantic co=
rrectness of the generated code. The Runnable but Incorrect Rate (RIR) are =
also increasing significantly, e.g., the GPT-4 model increases the incorrec=
t behaviors of the produced code from 24% in the original descriptions to 5=
4%, 65% and 89% in the documents. cases of Incomplete, Ambiguous and contra=
dictory descriptions. This indicates major issues with the semantic correct=
ness of the generated code even for the cases that the LLMs managed to prod=
uce some syntactically valid code.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" styl=
e=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS2.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">Interestingly, the relative impact of quality issues var=
ies between benchmarks <cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Chen e=
t&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v=
1#bib.bib8" title=3D"">2025</a>)</cite>. In HumanEval, ambiguous descriptio=
ns tend to degrade performance more than incomplete ones, whereas on MBPP, =
we observe the opposite. This may be due to the nature of the tasks: HumanE=
val problems typically require deeper reasoning, making ambiguity more impo=
rtant, while MBPP tasks rely more on explicitly defined operations, where m=
issing details are vital.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"disp=
lay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<figure class=3D"ltx_table" id=3D"S6.T2">
