<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S6.SS3">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>RQ3 =E2=80=93 Error =
Type Analysis</h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;"=
>Report issue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS3.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">To better understand the nature of model failures under =
unclear requirements, we analyze not only whether a generated solution runs=
 successfully, but also whether it produces the correct logic. These findin=
gs demonstrate that unclear requirements not only degrade task performance =
but also alter the nature of failure modes <cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_cite=
macro_citep">(Dou et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.o=
rg/html/2507.20439v1#bib.bib12" title=3D"">2024a</a>)</cite>. Although all =
models maintained a high Successful Execution Rate (SER) across task descri=
ptions conditions, this metric alone is insufficient. As shown in Figure&nb=
sp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#S6.F6" =
title=3D"Figure 6 =E2=80=A3 6.2. RQ2 =E2=80=93 Model Robustness =E2=80=A3 6=
. Results =E2=80=A3 When Prompts Go Wrong: Evaluating Code Model Robustness=
 to Ambiguous, Contradictory, and Incomplete Task Descriptions"><span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the Runnable but Incorrect Rate (RI=
R) increases significantly as description clarity declines. This means mode=
ls often produce syntactically valid code that compiles and executes, yet f=
ails the functional requirements of the task=E2=80=94suggesting deep semant=
ic misunderstandings. For instance, under contradictory task descriptions, =
RIR exceeds 80(%) for GPT-4 and approaches similar levels for other large m=
odels. In the case of ambiguous task descriptions, RIR remains high across =
all LLMs, with some small models such as Qwen-7B reaching over 70(%). These=
 results reinforce that execution alone is not a reliable proxy for correct=
ness, especially when task descriptions contain subtle or conflicting defec=
ts. To further unpack these failures, we analyzed the types of exceptions t=
hrown by incorrect generations using our largest LLMs. We extract 3114 erro=
rs and categorize them in Table&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://a=
rxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#S6.T4" title=3D"Table 4 =E2=80=A3 6.3. RQ3 =E2=
=80=93 Error Type Analysis =E2=80=A3 6. Results =E2=80=A3 When Prompts Go W=
rong: Evaluating Code Model Robustness to Ambiguous, Contradictory, and Inc=
omplete Task Descriptions"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a=
>, showing the distribution of common Python exception types across each de=
scriptions category. Our findings reveal that:</p><button class=3D"sr-only =
button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button=
>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS3.p2">
<ul class=3D"ltx_itemize" id=3D"S6.I1">
<li class=3D"ltx_item" id=3D"S6.I1.i1" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.I1.i1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incomplete task d=
escriptions:</span> are particularly prone to fundamental runtime failures =
<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Atiyaa and Al-Jawaherry, <a c=
lass=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.20439v1#bib.bib4" titl=
e=3D"">2025</a>)</cite> such as NotFoundError (82.4(%)), TypeError (37.3(%)=
), and SyntaxError (41.3(%)). These reflect structural gaps in the task des=
cription, often leading models to hallucinate or mishandle variables, argum=
ents, or input types.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display:=
 none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</li>
<li class=3D"ltx_item" id=3D"S6.I1.i2" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.I1.i2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ambiguous task de=
scriptions:</span> in contrast, tend to generate semantically flawed logic =
that nonetheless executes. These are marked by elevated AttributeError (31.=
4(%)) and KeyError (38.9(%)), which typically emerge from misinterpreting v=
ague specifications or assuming incorrect object structures.</p><button cla=
ss=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding e=
lement</button>
</div>
</li>
<li class=3D"ltx_item" id=3D"S6.I1.i3" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.I1.i3.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contradictory tas=
k descriptions</span> result in a mixed profile of errors, with moderate ra=
tes of logical faults across most categories, but notably higher NameError =
and ValueError rates. This suggests models are confused by internal inconsi=
stencies, leading to conflicting variable usage or return semantics.</p><bu=
tton class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for pre=
ceding element</button>
</div>
</li>
<li class=3D"ltx_item" id=3D"S6.I1.i4" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.I1.i4.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Original task des=
criptions</span>, though well formed, still yield a notable share of NameEr=
ror (29.8(%)) and ValueError (30.1(%)). Interestingly, these are less commo=
n in ambiguous task descriptions, possibly because detailed instructions co=
nstrain the model=E2=80=99s solution path, making small mistakes more impac=
tful, while ambiguous task descriptions allow greater structural flexibilit=
y.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issu=
e for preceding element</button>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.SS3.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p">These findings demonstrate that unclear requirements not=
 only degrade task performance but also alter the nature of failure modes. =
Incomplete task descriptions are more likely to trigger structural issues s=
uch as syntax and type errors; ambiguous task descriptions often result in =
semantically misaligned outputs; and contradictory task descriptions tend t=
o produce logically inconsistent or invalid solutions. This highlights the =
importance of advancing LLMs=E2=80=99 ability to reason under unclear instr=
uctions, a critical step for safe and effective integration into real-world=
 software development workflows where requirement clarity cannot be guarant=
eed.
<br class=3D"ltx_break"></p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"displ=
ay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
